      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that:\n1. The least common multiple (LCM) of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 36.\n2. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 6.\n\nLet's denote the two numbers as \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). For any two integers, the relationship between their LCM and GCD is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = 36\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the relationship formula, we substitute the given values:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\\[\n216 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\n\\]\nand\n\\[\na \\times b = 216\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\\), both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be multiples of 6. Let \\(a = 6m\\) and \\(b = 6n\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime integers (i.e., \\(\\text{GCD}(m, n) = 1\\)).\n\nSubstitute \\(a = 6m\\) and \\(b = 6n\\) into the product equation:\n\\[\na \\times b = (6m) \\times (6n) = 36mn\n\\]\n\\[\n36mn = 216\n\\]\n\\[\nmn = \\frac{216}{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find pairs \\((m, n)\\) such that \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime and their product is 6. The pairs of coprime factors of 6 are:\n\\[\n(1, 6), (2, 3), (3, 2), (6, 1)\n\\]\n\nWe will check each pair:\n1. For \\(m = 1\\) and \\(n = 6\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 6 \\times 1 = 6, \\quad b = 6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n   Check LCM:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM}(6, 36) = 36\n   \\]\n   Check GCD:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(6, 36) = 6\n   \\]\n   Both conditions are satisfied.\n\n2. For \\(m = 2\\) and \\(n = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 6 \\times 2 = 12, \\quad b = 6 \\times 3 = 18\n   \\]\n   Check LCM:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM}(12, 18) = \\text{LCM}(6 \\times 2, 6 \\times 3) = 36\n   \\]\n   Check GCD:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(12, 18) = \\text{GCD}(6 \\times 2, 6 \\times 3) = 6\n   \\]\n   Both conditions are satisfied.\n\n3. For \\(m = 3\\) and \\(n = 2\\):\n   This is the same as the pair \\((2, 3)\\), so it is also satisfied.\n\n4. For \\(m = 6\\) and \\(n = 1\\):\n   This is the same as the pair \\((1, 6)\\), so it is also satisfied.\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) that satisfy both conditions are:\n\\[\n(6, 36), (12, 18), (18, 12), (36, 6)\n\\]\n\nThe product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n6 \\times 36 = 216\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]",
