      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of the least common multiple (LCM) and the greatest common divisor (GCD). Let's denote the two integers by \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nWe are given:\n1. The least common multiple (LCM) of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 36.\n2. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 6.\n\nWe know the relationship between the LCM and GCD of two numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values into the equation:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = 216\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\na \\times b = 216\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the pairs of integers \\((a, b)\\) whose product is 216 and whose GCD is 6. Let's denote the two integers by \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Since \\(\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\\), we can express \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) as:\n\\[\na = 6m \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 6n\n\\]\nwhere \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime integers (i.e., \\(\\text{GCD}(m, n) = 1\\)).\n\nSubstituting these into the product equation:\n\\[\n(6m) \\times (6n) = 216\n\\]\n\\[\n36mn = 216\n\\]\n\\[\nmn = \\frac{216}{36}\n\\]\n\\[\nmn = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find all pairs of coprime integers \\((m, n)\\) such that \\(mn = 6\\). The factor pairs of 6 are:\n\\[\n(1, 6), (2, 3), (3, 2), (6, 1)\n\\]\n\nAmong these pairs, the only pair where both numbers are coprime is \\((2, 3)\\) and \\((3, 2)\\). Therefore, the possible pairs \\((a, b)\\) are:\n\\[\n(a, b) = (6 \\times 2, 6 \\times 3) = (12, 18) \\quad \\text{or} \\quad (a, b) = (6 \\times 3, 6 \\times 2) = (18, 12)\n\\]\n\nIn both cases, the product \\(a \\times b\\) is:\n\\[\n12 \\times 18 = 216\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]",
